1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:05,680
A little after seven on the evening of the
20th of September 1944, four British

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:10,340
tanks rolled north across the Great Road
Bridge at Nijmegen in the Netherlands.

3
00:00:11,180 --> 00:00:15,900
A few hours earlier, American paratroopers
had paid for that bridge by rowing across

4
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:20,120
the river wall in canvas boats under fire
in broad daylight.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:26,620
Eleven kilometers further up the same road
at another bridge in the town of Arnhem, a

6
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,340
few hundred British paratroopers were
still holding on.

7
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:31,700
They had been holding for three days.

8
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:34,400
They were waiting for exactly these tanks.

9
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:40,140
The tanks stopped in the next village, a
place called Lent, and that night the men

10
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:41,760
at Arnhem lost their bridge.

11
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:47,040
Decades later, the German general who had
been facing those four tanks was asked

12
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:47,820
about that evening.

13
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:51,380
The four tanks made a mistake when they
stayed in Lent.

14
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:55,160
If they had carried on, it would have been
all over for us.

15
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:57,220
That's an old man remembering.

16
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:02,120
An old Old men remembered generously, but
his own artillery map from the battle

17
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:07,000
shows almost nothing between Lent and
Arnhem except a few pickets with rifles.

18
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:11,360
Everybody who survived Operation Market
Garden had an explanation

19
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:12,600
for what went wrong.

20
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:14,520
Montgomery blamed the weather.

21
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:19,480
The American in charge of the airborne
army, Lewis Brereton, blamed

22
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:20,740
the slow British tanks.

23
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:26,060
Others blamed a trader, or a stack of
photographs that nobody wanted to look at,

24
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:30,380
or a German an army that was supposed to
be finished and somehow wasn't.

25
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:36,020
And Montgomery himself, when it was all
over, called the operation 90% successful.

26
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:38,180
So here's tonight's question.

27
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,020
Was Market Garden completely avoidable?

28
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:44,540
Not the war in the West, and not the
advance into Holland.

29
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:48,100
The Defeat at the Last Bridge Sir, it's
one road.

30
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:50,540
How many ways can you get lost on one
road?

31
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:51,540
On this road?

32
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:52,300
Let's count.

33
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:53,960
Every turn-off gets assigned.

34
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,800
Five bridges, two armies, one road.

35
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:57,920
Here are the rules.

36
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:01,660
Every exit on this road gets a date, a
place, and a person who

37
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:02,520
could have taken it.

38
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:06,120
If a turnoff only exists in hindsight, it
doesn't count.

39
00:02:06,770 --> 00:02:10,970
The allies get a column on the board, and
so do the Germans, because they missed

40
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:11,890
turnings too.

41
00:02:12,410 --> 00:02:17,110
And because this whole plan ran on a
timetable, our dispatcher keeps the clock.

42
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,840
Timetable says the tanks reach Arnhem in
two to three days.

43
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:25,720
Clock starts on a Sunday, and one sign on
this road, we won't paint at all.

44
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:26,640
You'll see which.

45
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:31,440
To understand why anyone thought this
could work, start with the summer that

46
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:32,360
made it look easy.

47
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:36,780
In August 1944, the German army in France
fell apart.

48
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:42,220
After the breakout from Normandy and the
pocket at Falaise, the Allies chased it

49
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:45,820
across France and Belgium faster than
their own maps could keep up.

50
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:51,000
Between early June and the middle of
August, The Germans in the West had lost

51
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,980
nearly 200,000 men missing or captured, on
top of the dead and wounded.

52
00:02:56,620 --> 00:03:01,040
On the 4th of September, British tanks
rolled into Antwerp, one of the biggest

53
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:04,500
ports in Europe, and found its docks
almost intact.

54
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:08,760
In Allied headquarters, the mood was
something close to a fever.

55
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:14,700
The Supreme Allied Command's intelligence
staff counted 48 German divisions in the

56
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:18,960
West, and reckoned they were worth about
20 infantry divisions

57
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:20,420
and four armored ones.

58
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:25,520
A British airborne staff calculated that
the whole of the Netherlands held no more

59
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:28,060
than 50 to 100 German tanks.

60
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:32,300
Looking back afterwards, the British
Airborne Division admitted

61
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,280
what it had assumed.

62
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:39,680
An enemy still disorganized after a long,
hasty retreat scattered in small groups

63
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,120
and not capable of much organized
resistance.

64
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:47,020
There was just one problem, and it was the
oldest one in war, fuel.

65
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:52,030
The petrol was sitting in Normandy,
hundreds of kilometers behind the front,

66
00:03:52,270 --> 00:03:54,310
and there weren't enough trucks to bring
it forward.

67
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:56,720
Antwerp could have fixed that.

68
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:02,120
Its docks were in allied hands, but the
long estuary that connects Antwerp to the

69
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:05,560
sea, the Schelt, was still held by the
Germans on both banks.

70
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:09,520
A port you can't sail into is just a very
large car park.

71
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:11,540
The Admiral saw it at once.

72
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:16,100
Admiral Andrew Cunningham warned that
unless the approaches were cleared,

73
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:19,200
Antwerp would be about as much use as
Timbuktu.

74
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:24,660
And while the Allied armies looked east
toward Germany, a whole German army, the

75
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:28,560
15th, was quietly escaping across that
estuary behind them.

76
00:04:29,030 --> 00:04:34,990
Between the 5th and the 21st of September,
some 65,000 men got across, with

77
00:04:34,990 --> 00:04:38,150
hundreds of guns and trucks, on anything
that floated.

78
00:04:38,810 --> 00:04:40,830
The Germans were doing something else,
too.

79
00:04:40,830 --> 00:04:46,310
Along the Albert Canal in Belgium, a
general named Kurt Chil, retreating with

80
00:04:46,310 --> 00:04:49,170
the remains of three divisions, simply
stopped.

81
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:53,290
He set up rallying points and grabbed
every straggler who came past.

82
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:58,310
Sailors, airmen, occupation officials, men
from almost every branch.

83
00:04:58,690 --> 00:05:01,750
Within hours, he had something that looked
like a front line.

84
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:03,070
It wasn't pretty.

85
00:05:03,410 --> 00:05:04,410
It didn't need to be.

86
00:05:04,830 --> 00:05:06,150
It only needed to exist.

87
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:06,290
thrust.

88
00:05:06,490 --> 00:05:11,170
On the Allied side, the argument was about
where to spend the last of the petrol.

89
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:14,230
Eisenhower wanted to advance on a broad
front.

90
00:05:14,710 --> 00:05:19,390
Montgomery wanted one concentrated thrust
in the north, under his command through

91
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:24,890
Holland, and around the end of Germany's
border defenses, the West Wall, straight

92
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:27,750
into the Ruhr, the industrial heart of the
Reich.

93
00:05:28,290 --> 00:05:32,770
And sitting in England, unused, was the
perfect tool for a thrust like that.

94
00:05:33,390 --> 00:05:37,250
Since Since the summer, Allied planners
had drawn up 18 different

95
00:05:37,250 --> 00:05:38,590
airborne operations.

96
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:44,320
Every single one had been cancelled,
usually because the ground troops overran

97
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:47,260
the objective before the paratroopers
could take off.

98
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:50,760
Washington wanted to see what a big
airborne army could do

99
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:52,280
deep behind enemy lines.

100
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,520
The paratroopers wanted to fight.

101
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:56,860
The tool was looking for a job.

102
00:05:57,280 --> 00:05:59,380
Sir, why does Antwerp get a sign?

103
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:01,140
We haven't even started driving.

104
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:04,940
Because the cheapest way to lose at Arnhem
is to go to Arnhem.

105
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:09,160
The first exit isn't on the road at all,
it's the road nobody took.

106
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:12,000
So which exit did we just drive past?

107
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:13,280
Antwerp first.

108
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:18,100
Clear the Sheldon September while the 15th
army was still on the wrong side of it,

109
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:20,400
and there is no market garden to lose.

110
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:22,000
People said so at the time.

111
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:23,060
That's one.

112
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:24,320
Allied Column.

113
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:29,980
On the 10th of September, Montgomery met
Eisenhower at Brussels Airport, inside the

114
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:31,260
Supreme commander's plane.

115
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:33,200
It was not a calm meeting.

116
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:38,020
Montgomery tore up a file of Eisenhower's
messages in front of him and demanded

117
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:42,920
priority for his northern thrust, in
language fierce enough that Eisenhower had

118
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:43,640
to stop him.

119
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:47,440
But by the end of it, Eisenhower had
approved the plan.

120
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:52,340
A smaller airborne scheme called Comet,
canceled that same day, was reborn bigger,

121
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:54,100
bolder and with a new name.

122
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:56,020
Market for the airborne part.

123
00:06:56,460 --> 00:06:57,620
Garden for the tanks.

124
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:01,820
Eisenhower gave it priority, but only
limited priority.

125
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:06,860
The next day, Montgomery wrote back
warning that without full priority, the

126
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:12,680
attack couldn't go in before the 23rd,
maybe the 26th of September, and then he

127
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:14,240
added a sentence worth keeping.

128
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,080
This delay will give the enemy time to
organize better defensive arrangements,

129
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:23,040
and we must expect heavier resistance and
slower progress.

130
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:28,040
Eisenhower sent his chief of staff to
promise a thousand tons of supplies a day

131
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:30,220
by plane and by American truck.

132
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:34,460
Montgomery was delighted and pulled the
date forward to Sunday,

133
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,140
the 17th of September.

134
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:40,280
That gave the Airborne Army seven days to
plan the largest airborne

135
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:41,500
operation in history.

136
00:07:42,530 --> 00:07:46,390
Earlier airborne attacks had been planned
for weeks, sometimes months.

137
00:07:46,810 --> 00:07:47,970
So is that an exit, sir?

138
00:07:48,330 --> 00:07:48,970
Take more time?

139
00:07:49,450 --> 00:07:49,690
Hmm.

140
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:53,280
Every day you spend planning is a day the
Germans spend digging.

141
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:56,020
That's not a turn-off, that's a toll
either way.

142
00:07:56,420 --> 00:07:56,900
No sign.

143
00:07:57,320 --> 00:07:59,220
Here's what the seven days produced.

144
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:04,500
Three and a half Airborne divisions would
drop along a single road running north

145
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:05,660
from the Belgian border.

146
00:08:06,700 --> 00:08:10,480
The American 101st would take the bridges
around Eindhoven.

147
00:08:10,860 --> 00:08:14,720
The American 82nd would take the bridges
at Grave and Nijmegen.

148
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:16,940
And the high ground beside them.

149
00:08:17,510 --> 00:08:22,610
And the British 1st Airborne division with
a Polish parachute brigade behind it would

150
00:08:22,610 --> 00:08:27,490
take the bridge over the lower Rhine at
Arnhem, the farthest one of all.

151
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:32,770
Then the tanks of the British 30 Corps
under Lieutenant General Brian Horrocks

152
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:38,110
would drive up that road across that
carpet of paratroopers, cross the Rhine,

153
00:08:38,350 --> 00:08:38,990
and keep going.

154
00:08:39,410 --> 00:08:40,790
And it really was one road.

155
00:08:41,190 --> 00:08:43,170
The Dutch countryside there is polder.

156
00:08:43,490 --> 00:08:48,550
Flat, wet fields cut by ditches and dykes,
with the highway often raised above them

157
00:08:48,550 --> 00:08:49,330
like a causeway.

158
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:51,280
Tanks that left it sank.

159
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:55,660
Horrocks told his officers he intended to
push 20,000 vehicles up

160
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:57,240
that road in 60 hours.

161
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:02,040
In private, according to an American
liaison officer, he was asking

162
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:03,060
different questions.

163
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:05,800
How many days rations will they jump with?

164
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:07,720
How long can they hold out?

165
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:09,540
Then came the aircraft.

166
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:14,280
There were only enough planes to carry
about half the airborne troops at once.

167
00:09:15,340 --> 00:09:19,600
The division commanders asked for two
flights on the first day, so that everyone

168
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,660
would be on the ground before the Germans
could react.

169
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:24,680
The troop carrier commanders refused.

170
00:09:25,320 --> 00:09:29,380
Two missions in one day meant no time for
repairs and exhausted crews.

171
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,360
Brereton sided with the airmen, one lift a
day, delivering everybody

172
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:35,420
would take four days.

173
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:39,920
For the British at Arnhem, that decision
was multiplied by geography.

174
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:45,700
The commander, Major General Roy Urquhart,
wanted landing zones close to the bridge.

175
00:09:46,100 --> 00:09:49,920
But the city was It's full of
anti-aircraft guns, and the ground south

176
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:53,320
of the river was judged too soft and too
cut up by dykes.

177
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:58,160
So the drop zones went in Heathland west
of Arnhem, up to 13

178
00:09:58,160 --> 00:09:59,380
kilometers from the bridge.

179
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:04,200
And since half the division wouldn't
arrive until the next day, half of the

180
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:07,880
half that landed first would have to stay
behind and guard the fields

181
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:08,820
for the second lift.

182
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:10,440
On day one,

183
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:12,990
one brigade would walk to the bridge.

184
00:10:12,990 --> 00:10:17,710
Sir, my set reaches about 5 km in good
weather, and the

185
00:10:17,710 --> 00:10:19,570
bridge is 13 away, noted.

186
00:10:20,430 --> 00:10:23,890
That's the main British radio of the
division, the Type 22 set.

187
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:26,840
Its effective range was about 5 km.

188
00:10:27,930 --> 00:10:30,570
Remember that radio, it's going to keep
coming back.

189
00:10:30,890 --> 00:10:33,510
So, which exits did we just drive past?

190
00:10:34,030 --> 00:10:35,610
Two of them, both in England.

191
00:10:36,110 --> 00:10:40,230
One lift a day, when the division
commanders asked for two, and dropped

192
00:10:40,230 --> 00:10:43,690
zones that put a long walk between the
paratroopers and the one

193
00:10:43,690 --> 00:10:44,950
bridge that mattered most.

194
00:10:45,490 --> 00:10:47,110
That's three, Allied Column.

195
00:10:47,690 --> 00:10:52,630
Now crossed to the other side of the road,
because the Germans were making plans too,

196
00:10:53,110 --> 00:10:54,870
and getting things wrong of their own.

197
00:10:55,610 --> 00:10:59,970
On the 3rd of September, a week before
anyone in the Allied camp had even

198
00:10:59,970 --> 00:11:04,590
sketched Market Garden, Field Marshal
Walter Model made a routine decision.

199
00:11:05,130 --> 00:11:10,570
Model commanded German Army Group B in the
West, and he sent two battered SS Panzer

200
00:11:10,570 --> 00:11:15,550
divisions, the 9th and the 10th, to the
area around Arnhem to rest and refit.

201
00:11:15,890 --> 00:11:19,830
Two days later, he sent their corps
headquarters under General Wilhelm

202
00:11:19,830 --> 00:11:21,050
Bittrich to join them.

203
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:25,140
These were famous names, Hohenstaufen and
Frunsberg.

204
00:11:25,580 --> 00:11:28,380
They were also shadows of themselves after
Normandy.

205
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:33,480
One British historian estimates the whole
corps was down to perhaps six or seven

206
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:36,300
thousand men, a fraction of its proper
strength.

207
00:11:36,770 --> 00:11:41,670
Some of their units were already packing
to leave for Germany, and Model himself

208
00:11:41,670 --> 00:11:46,510
set up his headquarters in a pleasant
hotel in Oosterbeek, a leafy village on

209
00:11:46,510 --> 00:11:47,830
the western edge of Arnhem.

210
00:11:48,290 --> 00:11:49,490
Keep an eye on that hotel.

211
00:11:50,070 --> 00:11:52,010
German intelligence was not asleep.

212
00:11:52,570 --> 00:11:57,610
From the 9th to the 14th of September,
Model's intelligence officer warned every

213
00:11:57,610 --> 00:12:02,930
day that a British offensive was coming,
aimed toward Nijmegen, Arnhem, and Wessel,

214
00:12:03,250 --> 00:12:04,710
with the Ruhr as the prize.

215
00:12:04,710 --> 00:12:07,470
On the ground, he was almost exactly
right,

216
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:12,120
but when it came to the Airborne Army, the
Germans looked in the wrong place.

217
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:16,060
They expected paratroopers far to the east
of the Rhine.

218
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:20,660
Model, after reports of landing craft
gathering in British ports, became

219
00:12:20,660 --> 00:12:24,460
convinced that a seaburn sea invasion of
Northern Holland was coming.

220
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:30,260
Even as the transport planes were in the
air on the 17th, the German commander in

221
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:34,640
the west was ordering a study of possible
landings on the Dutch coast.

222
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:37,640
which brings us to the first myth on this
road.

223
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:43,220
After the war, a counterintelligence
officer named Oreste Pinto claimed that

224
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:44,900
Market Garden had been betrayed.

225
00:12:45,610 --> 00:12:50,770
A Dutch resistance man called Christian
Lindemans, nicknamed King Kong, had

226
00:12:50,770 --> 00:12:54,870
supposedly told the Germans the plan, and
that was why the SS Panzers

227
00:12:54,870 --> 00:12:56,030
were waiting at Arnhem.

228
00:12:56,390 --> 00:12:57,890
So it was a traitor, sir?

229
00:12:58,530 --> 00:12:59,890
The traitor might be real.

230
00:13:00,330 --> 00:13:02,250
The betrayal doesn't fit the timetable.

231
00:13:02,670 --> 00:13:03,510
Here's the paperwork.

232
00:13:03,510 --> 00:13:03,690
work.

233
00:13:04,230 --> 00:13:09,850
Lindemans was arrested in October 1944 and
killed himself in his cell in 1946

234
00:13:09,850 --> 00:13:15,090
before he could be tried, and a French
