corridor far to the south in the American

463
00:26:52,740 --> 00:26:56,500
sector, and tanks had to turn back to help
reopen it.

464
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:01,420
The highway the soldiers had started
calling Hell's Highway was living up to

465
00:27:01,420 --> 00:27:02,860
its name in both directions.

466
00:27:03,720 --> 00:27:07,840
That Thursday, the Polish Parachute
Brigade of Major General Stanisław

467
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:12,880
Sosabowski finally jumped, two days late
because of the weather, and not

468
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:13,740
where it had planned.

469
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:19,100
Its landing zone had changed to the polder
near the village of Driel, on the South

470
00:27:19,100 --> 00:27:20,760
Bank opposite the British.

471
00:27:21,940 --> 00:27:24,240
114 transports set out from England.

472
00:27:24,760 --> 00:27:26,040
41 turned back.

473
00:27:26,540 --> 00:27:28,140
About a thousand poles landed.

474
00:27:28,920 --> 00:27:32,540
The ferry they were supposed to use to
cross the river was out of action.

475
00:27:33,270 --> 00:27:37,850
And how did the British, a few hundred
meters away across the water, tell the

476
00:27:37,850 --> 00:27:38,870
Poles what was happening?

477
00:27:39,370 --> 00:27:40,170
Not by radio.

478
00:27:40,610 --> 00:27:42,010
The set still couldn't reach them.

479
00:27:42,830 --> 00:27:46,630
A British private named Ernest Archer swam
the Rhine with a message.

480
00:27:47,210 --> 00:27:49,570
Sir, that's further than my set reaches
too.

481
00:27:50,110 --> 00:27:50,410
Yes.

482
00:27:50,890 --> 00:27:53,970
On this road, the most reliable radio was
a man who could swim.

483
00:27:54,780 --> 00:27:58,980
North of the river, what was left of the
British division had been squeezed into a

484
00:27:58,980 --> 00:28:03,020
pocket around Oosterbeek, the same village
where Model's Hotel stood.

485
00:28:03,970 --> 00:28:08,710
Aid posts sat in the front line in houses
that changed hands more than once.

486
00:28:09,460 --> 00:28:14,340
Resupply planes kept dropping ammunition
and food onto landing zones the Germans

487
00:28:14,340 --> 00:28:15,140
now held.

488
00:28:16,120 --> 00:28:20,620
On the night of the 24th, a battalion of
the Dorsetshire Regiment tried to cross

489
00:28:20,620 --> 00:28:22,140
the river to reinforce them.

490
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:28,460
Of the 315 men who set out, about 200
ended up as prisoners, waters, and only

491
00:28:28,460 --> 00:28:30,520
a handful reached the British lines.

492
00:28:31,100 --> 00:28:35,500
At half past nine on the morning of the
25th, Horrocks and Browning agreed to

493
00:28:35,500 --> 00:28:36,820
bring the survivors back.

494
00:28:37,420 --> 00:28:42,780
That night, in heavy rain, with their
boots muffled with cloth, the airborne men

495
00:28:42,780 --> 00:28:45,340
slipped down to the river through the
German lines.

496
00:28:45,900 --> 00:28:50,600
Engineers ferried them across in small
boats until daylight forced them to stop.

497
00:28:51,340 --> 00:28:53,800
About 2,400 men came back.

498
00:28:54,200 --> 00:28:59,410
Roughly 300 were left on the North Bank
and nearly 100 were killed

499
00:28:59,410 --> 00:29:00,170
during the crossing.

500
00:29:00,690 --> 00:29:03,870
Speaking for his men, General Erkhart said
just this,

501
00:29:04,530 --> 00:29:05,490
we have no regrets.

502
00:29:06,190 --> 00:29:09,490
Someone had to be blamed though and it
wasn't going to be the planners.

503
00:29:10,590 --> 00:29:15,110
In October, Montgomery told the head of
the British Army that the Poles had fought

504
00:29:15,110 --> 00:29:16,090
very badly.

505
00:29:17,050 --> 00:29:21,190
Browning wrote a long letter attacking
Sosabowski as difficult and unwilling.

506
00:29:21,650 --> 00:29:25,430
In December, under British pressure, the
Polish government in exile

507
00:29:25,430 --> 00:29:26,810
removed him from command.

508
00:29:27,470 --> 00:29:29,850
Many historians now call that a disgrace.

509
00:29:30,610 --> 00:29:35,610
In 2006, the Netherlands gave the Polish
Brigade its highest military honor for

510
00:29:35,610 --> 00:29:40,550
gallantry, and Sosabowski a decoration of
his own, long after his death.

511
00:29:40,950 --> 00:29:43,750
So let's read the road back one sign at a
time.

512
00:29:44,110 --> 00:29:45,610
Antwerp, the road nobody took.

513
00:29:46,030 --> 00:29:46,290
Allied.

514
00:29:46,850 --> 00:29:49,330
Seven days to plan, a toll either way, no
sign.

515
00:29:49,730 --> 00:29:50,790
One lift a day, Allied.

516
00:29:51,310 --> 00:29:53,370
Drop zones 13 kilometers out, Allied.

517
00:29:53,370 --> 00:29:58,710
Bedell Smith's warning allied, the
Nijmegen bridge on day one allied, Grave

518
00:29:58,710 --> 00:30:02,910
left standing German, Nijmegen left
standing German, and Lent, blank.

519
00:30:03,370 --> 00:30:07,910
Five exits on the allied side, two on the
German side, one nobody can settle.

520
00:30:08,230 --> 00:30:10,810
So, was Market Garden completely
avoidable?

521
00:30:11,150 --> 00:30:12,010
Here's our verdict.

522
00:30:12,530 --> 00:30:16,310
The defeat at Arnhem was avoidable, but
not by one heroic decision.

523
00:30:16,960 --> 00:30:21,980
The plan needed every bridge, every day,
and every radio to work, and it had no

524
00:30:21,980 --> 00:30:26,520
spare time built in for the first thing
that didn't, give the British Division two

525
00:30:26,520 --> 00:30:30,160
lifts on the first day, or put it down
closer to the bridge,

526
00:30:30,900 --> 00:30:35,760
or listen to Bedel Smith and put more
strength at Arnhem, or take the Nijmegen

527
00:30:35,760 --> 00:30:39,820
Bridge on the first evening, and the race
on that road looks very different.

528
00:30:40,610 --> 00:30:42,490
Any one of those might have been enough.

529
00:30:42,970 --> 00:30:44,510
The Allies needed only one.

530
00:30:44,970 --> 00:30:45,630
They took none.

531
00:30:46,150 --> 00:30:48,250
What wasn't anyone's fault was the luck.

532
00:30:48,250 --> 00:30:52,630
The SS divisions were sent to Arnhem a
week before the plan existed.

533
00:30:53,150 --> 00:30:54,470
No traitor put them there.

534
00:30:54,890 --> 00:31:00,010
And the Germans, too, made mistakes that
kept the operation alive, leaving two

535
00:31:00,010 --> 00:31:02,690
great bridges standing that they could
have blown.

536
00:31:03,110 --> 00:31:06,710
Now, to be fair, historians don't agree on
where the weight lies.

537
00:31:07,520 --> 00:31:10,700
The American official history, written in
1963,

538
00:31:11,520 --> 00:31:16,160
found the plan itself drew little
criticism and argued that its handicaps

539
00:31:16,160 --> 00:31:19,280
could have been overcome if the ground
column had moved as

540
00:31:19,280 --> 00:31:20,820
fast as Horrocks hoped.

541
00:31:21,460 --> 00:31:26,200
Many later writers put the blame squarely
on the plan, the distant drop zones, the

542
00:31:26,200 --> 00:31:28,040
single daily lift, the radios.

543
00:31:28,840 --> 00:31:33,100
Others stress how fast the Germans
recovered, how a beaten army rebuilt what

544
00:31:33,100 --> 00:31:38,760
one British historian calls a crust,
sagraggers and training battalions in less

545
00:31:38,760 --> 00:31:44,260
than two weeks, and Montgomery, who called
it 90% successful, later

546
00:31:44,260 --> 00:31:46,500
admitted something closer to the truth.

547
00:31:46,860 --> 00:31:48,620
It was a bad mistake on my part.

548
00:31:48,900 --> 00:31:53,520
I underestimated the difficulties of
opening up the approaches to Antwerp, and

549
00:31:53,520 --> 00:31:56,880
that famous line that the plan was going a
bridge too far?

550
00:31:57,510 --> 00:32:01,050
It's usually credited to Browning, said to
Montgomery before the battle.

551
00:32:01,550 --> 00:32:02,590
It's a great line.

552
00:32:03,220 --> 00:32:07,040
We couldn't pin it to anything written
down at the time, so it stays in the

553
00:32:07,040 --> 00:32:08,640
glovebox, unsigned.

554
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:10,160
The bill was heavy.

555
00:32:10,720 --> 00:32:15,080
By the official American count, the
airborne part of the operation alone cost

556
00:32:15,080 --> 00:32:17,020
close to 12,000 casualties.

557
00:32:17,740 --> 00:32:23,200
The British airborne troops lost more than
7,000 men killed, wounded, or missing.

558
00:32:23,720 --> 00:32:25,940
The ground corps added thousands more.

559
00:32:26,460 --> 00:32:32,400
A German signal after the battle reported
around 3,300 casualties of their own

560
00:32:32,400 --> 00:32:34,620
around Arnhem, and the Dutch paid.

561
00:32:34,860 --> 00:32:40,040
Eindhoven was bombed by the Luftwaffe
during the battle, and more than 200 of

562
00:32:40,040 --> 00:32:41,000
its people were killed.

563
00:32:41,600 --> 00:32:45,400
To help the offensive, Dutch railway
workers went on strike.

564
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:50,260
In reply, the German occupiers stopped
food shipments into the west of the

565
00:32:50,260 --> 00:32:55,860
country, and in the hunger winter that
followed, more than 20,000 Dutch civilians

566
00:32:55,860 --> 00:32:57,340
starved to death.

567
00:32:57,740 --> 00:32:58,360
Antwerp,

568
00:32:59,130 --> 00:33:00,950
the port that could have been opened in
September,

569
00:33:01,650 --> 00:33:04,290
received its first convoy on the 28th of
November.

570
00:33:04,290 --> 00:33:09,640
Prince Bernhard of the Netherlands is
reported to have said that his country

571
00:33:09,640 --> 00:33:13,500
could never again afford the luxury of
another Montgomery success.

572
00:33:14,410 --> 00:33:15,750
That's our reading of the road.

573
00:33:16,230 --> 00:33:16,730
What's yours?

574
00:33:17,520 --> 00:33:21,720
At Lent on the night of the 20th of
September, would you have sent the four

575
00:33:21,720 --> 00:33:24,840
tanks on to Arnhem or held them until
morning?

576
00:33:25,780 --> 00:33:30,180
Tell us in the comments and tell us which
sign you would have painted and if you

577
00:33:30,180 --> 00:33:33,440
want more history from both sides of the
road, subscribe.

578
00:33:34,220 --> 00:33:37,100
It's one lift and you don't have to wait
for the second day.

579
00:33:37,640 --> 00:33:39,960
The bridge at Arnhem didn't survive the
war.

580
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:45,980
On the 7th of October, 1944, American
bombers destroyed it to keep the Germans

581
00:33:45,980 --> 00:33:46,800
from using it.

582
00:33:47,120 --> 00:33:50,100
A new bridge went up in the same place
after the war.

583
00:33:50,560 --> 00:33:51,580
Since 1977,

584
00:33:52,300 --> 00:33:56,680
it has carried the name of the man who got
there and held it, the John Frost Bridge.

585
00:33:57,200 --> 00:34:00,280
Final entry, planned arrival at Arnhem day
two or three.

586
00:34:00,700 --> 00:34:02,840
Actual arrival, never, close the log.

